\title{How to read the benchmark tables}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{benchmark}
\meta{origin}{bench/track/benchmarks-notes.md}
\meta{moved}{2026-11-24}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-25}
\import{base-macros}

\p{This text is written by hand and included by the generated tables, [the benchmark tables](rep-benchmarks); \src{bench/track/benchmarks.py} writes those and does not touch this tree.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Order.} Warm wall clock with 8 workers and the load pipelined, warm wall clock with 1 worker, cold wall clock of a fresh process, CPU time, peak resident memory, instructions: the order of [the standing policies](standing-policies). One table per metric; in every table \strong{the first row is the executable in its default configuration}, what \code{\verb~|rlvm FILE~} does with no option.}
\li{\strong{The engines and the contract of the default configuration.} RLVM has two engines in one executable. The \strong{machine} (\code{rlvm-machine}: the worklist machine, the engine the project is named after) is the default engine since 2026-11-25 03:05 UTC, with the switch set that \code{\verb~|rlvm --version~} prints. The \strong{reference engine} (\code{rlvm-ref}: the official kernel's algorithm on hash-consed terms) re-checks every rejection, decides every unit the machine declines, and alone it is the strict mode; its rows are runs with \code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~}. A row that was measured without \code{\verb~|-X engine~} belongs to the engine that was the default at the time of the row. Whatever engine is the default, the default configuration promises what [the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract) says: never an accept of a statement that is not derivable; the official verdict on every real input; everything the official checker accepts is accepted; a derivable statement that the official checker rejects only through its own implementation may be accepted.}
\li{\strong{Platforms.} The release is one executable, see [packaging](design-packaging): the \strong{executable} is the native image \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm~}, built with GraalVM Community Edition at \code{\verb~|-O3~} with the serial collector and the heap settings its \code{\verb~|rlvm --version~} prints; it is what a user runs and what the arena definition runs, and its rows are the ones that count. The \strong{optional Oracle GraalVM executable} is the same executable built with Oracle GraalVM and profile-guided optimisation from the profiles of the repository; it is an explicitly requested build under another licence and never a default, and its rows are labelled. The \strong{JVM launcher} is \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-jvm~} of a development distribution: the same jars on HotSpot with C2, the serial collector, 8 compiler threads and transparent huge pages, heap by engine. It is a tool of the repository, not part of a release; warm, a JIT is faster than the image, which is why its rows are kept.}
\li{\strong{Configuration of every RLVM row.} \code{\verb~|--threads 8~}, which is what the automatic count chooses on 8 cores, or \code{\verb~|--threads 1~}; no other option on a row of the default configuration: the load is pipelined with the checking, one scanner thread reads a file of 256 MiB or more, the image runs with the collection policy and the heap limit that are baked in, see [the settings of a plain run](dec-EGMG), and the JVM launcher with its own heap for the engine.}
\li{\strong{Where.} The aarch64 development host (Cortex-X925), the 8 measurement cores, every row inside a quiet-core window, one measurement at a time. RLVM runs on the host, gruszka and nano-lean in the arena-shaped container on the same 8 cores with their arena run lines and 8 or 1 threads. Two identical rows of 8 workers differ by up to 5 \%: smaller differences are not findings. The arena itself runs on x86-64 and ranks by instructions: absolute numbers differ.}
\li{\strong{The other cores: idle since the tables of 2026-11-25.} The rows of the build that has [the three changes of the optimisation wave](dec-JT59) were taken while the other 12 cores of the host were idle, and the rows of gruszka and nano-lean with 8 threads in the same windows as the executable's. The tables before that date were taken while the other 12 cores were busy with builds and gates, the baselines in windows of their own; that costs memory bandwidth and the shared cache, and it costs the baselines more than RLVM: gruszka's rows are 10 to 32 \% shorter now than then (Std 1.28 to 0.87 s, Mathlib 14.20 to 10.68 s), nano-lean's 6 to 23 \%, and the same RLVM executable is 4 to 14 \% faster on a quiet host. So a ratio to gruszka in these tables cannot be compared with one in the tables before; what the wave changed in RLVM, under one condition, is in [the report of the integration](rep-INT8), section 3. The condition now is that of the arena's runner, 8 cores with nothing else running. The rows of the baselines with one thread are still those of 2026-11-24.}
\li{\strong{Warm} means that the same process checks the same export again (\code{\verb~|rlvm -X passes=N~}); every pass is a complete run with a new store, a new loader and new engines, the load pipelined with the checking. The first pass of a JVM is its warm-up and is reported with the cold figures, not charged in the warm tables. \strong{Cold} is one fresh process from exec to exit.}
\li{\strong{Superseded.} The tables of 2026-11-24 and before, taken with the reference engine as the default, the machine's earlier switch sets and the image before its settings were chosen, are in [the first warm tables](rep-warm-wall-clock) and in the reports of the profilers. A cell here is the latest row of its kind, and the provenance at the end says for every cell which run, which window and which jars it is.}
}
